* START-HISTORY:
* 14 Aug 26 Windows port - replaces groupadd, groupdel, usermod -aG, deluser
* 15 Aug 26 Windows port - LISTMEM added for GRANT/REVOKE, 7 step 5e
* END-HISTORY
*
* START-DESCRIPTION:
*
*    rc = !os_group(action, group, member)
*
*    action  ADD     create the group           member ignored
*            DEL     delete the group           member ignored
*            ADDMEM  add member to the group
*            DELMEM  remove member from the group
*            LISTMEM list the group's members   member is an OUTPUT
*
*    LISTMEM RETURNS THROUGH "member", which is the only argument that can
*    carry a list back, and is why it is not validated on the way in - a
*    caller quite properly passes an empty variable.  The result is a field
*    mark delimited list of bare account names with any MACHINE\ prefix
*    stripped, so a name that comes out of LISTMEM can go straight back into
*    DELMEM.  An empty list and a group with no members are the same answer.
*
*    It exists because GRANT is Windows group membership (7 step 5): once the
*    grant stopped being a field in the ACCOUNTS record, "who may enter this
*    account" stopped being a question a dictionary item could answer, and an
*    account whose grants cannot be listed cannot be audited by eye.
*
*    group   a local group name, OR a security identifier written as
*            "S-1-5-32-544".  ADDMEM and DELMEM accept either; ADD and DEL
*            take a name only, since a group cannot be created with a chosen
*            SID.
*
*    returns 0 success
*            1 the operation failed
*            2 a name is not valid, or the action is not one of the above
*            5 access denied: SD is not running elevated
*
*    WHY A SID IS ACCEPTED AT ALL.  BUILTIN\Administrators is renamed on a
*    localised Windows - it is "Administratoren" on a German machine - so
*    naming it would work here and fail there.  Its SID never changes, and
*    Add-LocalGroupMember has a parameter set that takes one.  Anything
*    built in must be addressed this way; sd.iss writes *S-1-5-32-544 to
*    icacls for exactly the same reason.  Groups SD creates itself, sdusers
*    and the sdu_ ones, are our own names and are safe to write out.
*
*    WHY ONE SUBROUTINE RATHER THAN FOUR OS.EXECUTE LINES.  These four
*    operations appear at five call sites across CREATEA, DELACC and MODIFYA,
*    and every one of them needs the same elevation check and the same
*    quoting.  Section 5.14 asks for administrative capability to go in a
*    subroutine with something over it rather than in the caller, so that a
*    form can call the same code later instead of reimplementing it.
*
*    ADD and ADDMEM are idempotent: a group that already exists, or a member
*    already in it, is success rather than failure.  Account creation is
*    re-runnable that way, which matters because it can fail half finished -
*    the SD account and the Windows account are not created in one
*    transaction and nothing can make them so.
*
*    The Linux original passed "groupadd -U root,sdsys,<user>" to seed the
*    group's membership.  root and sdsys are Linux accounts with no Windows
*    counterpart, so only the SD user is added, by a separate ADDMEM call.
*
*    ELEVATION.  Local group changes need an elevated token; an ordinary SD
*    session has a UAC-filtered one and gets "System error 5, access is
*    denied".  Measured 14 Aug 2026.  The check is made explicitly rather
*    than read out of a failure message, because those are localised.
*
* END-DESCRIPTION
*
* START-CODE

$internal
function os_group(action, group, member)
$catalog !os_group

deffun valid_os_name(name) calling '!valid_os_name'

   rc = 2
   set.status 0

   if not(valid_os_name(group)) then return rc

   act = upcase(action)

* 15 Aug 26 Windows port - LISTMEM is deliberately absent from this test.
*   Its "member" is an output, and validating it would reject the empty
*   variable the caller is supposed to pass.
   if act = 'ADDMEM' or act = 'DELMEM' then
      if not(valid_os_name(member)) then return rc
   end

*  A SID rather than a name.  !valid_os_name already passed it - letters,
*  digits and hyphens - so it cannot carry a quote into the script below.
*  grp.sel selects the cmdlet parameter, so the four cases stay one line each.
   is.sid = (upcase(group)[1,4] = 'S-1-')
   if is.sid then
      if act = 'ADD' or act = 'DEL' then return rc  ;* cannot name a new group's SID
      grp.sel = '-SID "' : group : '"'
   end else
      grp.sel = '-Group "' : group : '"'
   end

*  14 Aug 26 Windows port - SH1 names PowerShell, so the command IS the
*  script and there is no bash layer to quote against.  Both names have been
*  through !valid_os_name - letters, digits, dot, underscore and hyphen only -
*  so neither can carry a quote out of the strings below.

   ps = 'if (-not (New-Object Security.Principal.WindowsPrincipal('
   ps := '[Security.Principal.WindowsIdentity]::GetCurrent())).IsInRole('
   ps := '[Security.Principal.WindowsBuiltInRole]::Administrator)) { exit 5 }; '

   begin case
      case act = 'ADD'
         ps := 'if (Get-LocalGroup -Name "' : group : '" -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue) { exit 0 }; '
         ps := 'try { New-LocalGroup -Name "' : group : '" -Description "SD account group" -ErrorAction Stop | Out-Null } catch { exit 1 }; exit 0'

      case act = 'DEL'
         ps := 'try { Remove-LocalGroup -Name "' : group : '" -ErrorAction Stop } catch { exit 1 }; exit 0'

      case act = 'ADDMEM'
         ps := 'try { Add-LocalGroupMember ' : grp.sel : ' -Member "' : member : '" -ErrorAction Stop } '
         ps := 'catch [Microsoft.PowerShell.Commands.MemberExistsException] { exit 0 } catch { exit 1 }; exit 0'

      case act = 'DELMEM'
         ps := 'try { Remove-LocalGroupMember ' : grp.sel : ' -Member "' : member : '" -ErrorAction Stop } catch { exit 1 }; exit 0'

*     15 Aug 26 Windows port - the split is done in PowerShell rather than in
*     BASIC, following !is_grp_member, which compares on the last
*     backslash-delimited component the same way.  It keeps a backslash out of
*     BASIC source, where it is also a string delimiter.
      case act = 'LISTMEM'
         ps := 'try { $ms = Get-LocalGroupMember ' : grp.sel : ' -ErrorAction Stop } catch { exit 1 }; '
         ps := 'foreach ($m in $ms) { ($m.Name -split "\\")[-1] }; exit 0'

      case 1
         return rc
   end case

   if act = 'LISTMEM' then
      os.execute ps capturing grp.out
   end else
      os.execute ps
   end

   rc = os.error()
   if rc # 0 then set.status rc

*  THE CARRIAGE RETURN HAS TO COME OFF, and TRIM does not take it - it removes
*  spaces.  PowerShell ends every line CRLF, the capture splits on the LF, and
*  each name arrives with a CR still on it.  Measured 15 Aug 2026: LIST.GRANTS
*  printed a blank line after every member, which looked like display untidiness
*  and was not.  A name carrying a CR fails !valid_os_name - letters, digits,
*  dot, underscore and hyphen only - so it comes back as status 2 from DELMEM,
*  and this routine's own promise that a LISTMEM name can be fed straight back
*  to DELMEM would have been false.
*
*  Blank lines are dropped rather than passed on: PowerShell emits a trailing
*  one, and a caller counting members with DCOUNT would count it.
   if act = 'LISTMEM' then
      member = ''
      if rc = 0 then
         lines = dcount(grp.out, @fm)
         for ln = 1 to lines
            nm = grp.out<ln>
            convert char(13) : char(10) to '' in nm
            nm = trim(nm)
            if nm # '' then member<-1> = nm
         next ln
      end
   end

return rc
end

* END-CODE
